      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\\) for the smallest positive angle \\(x\\), we can use trigonometric identities. We start by using the identity for the cosine of a sum:\n\n\\[\n\\cos(A + B) = \\cos A \\cos B - \\sin A \\sin B\n\\]\n\nApplying this identity to the right-hand side of the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 2x \\cos 3x - \\sin 2x \\sin 3x = 0\n\\]\n\nThis can be rewritten using the new trigonometric identity for the cosine of a difference:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 2x \\cos 3x - \\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos(2x + 3x) = \\cos 5x\n\\]\n\nSo the equation simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 5x = 0\n\\]\n\nThe cosine function is zero at odd multiples of \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) radians. Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n5x = \\frac{\\pi}{2} + k\\pi\n\\]\n\nfor any integer \\(k\\). To find the smallest positive angle \\(x\\), we set \\(k = 0\\) to get:\n\n\\[\n5x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{\\pi}{10}\n\\]\n\nConverting \\(\\pi/10\\) radians to degrees, we use the conversion factor \\(\\pi\\) radians = 180 degrees, so:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{\\pi}{10} \\times \\frac{180}{\\pi} = 18\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest positive angle \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{18}\n\\]",
