      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We start with the given equation \\(\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\\). Using the trigonometric identity for the cosine of a sum, we have:\n\\[\n\\cos (2x + 3x) = \\cos 5x.\n\\]\nThus, the given equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\cos 5x = \\sin 2x \\sin 3x - \\cos 2x \\cos 3x.\n\\]\nUsing the product-to-sum identities, we can rewrite \\(\\sin 2x \\sin 3x\\) and \\(\\cos 2x \\cos 3x\\) as follows:\n\\[\n\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (2x - 3x) - \\cos (2x + 3x)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (-x) - \\cos 5x] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos x - \\cos 5x],\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\cos 2x \\cos 3x = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (2x + 3x) + \\cos (2x - 3x)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 5x + \\cos x].\n\\]\nSubstituting these into the original equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\cos 5x = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos x - \\cos 5x] - \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 5x + \\cos x].\n\\]\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we obtain:\n\\[\n\\cos 5x = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos x - \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 5x - \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 5x - \\frac{1}{2} \\cos x = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 5x - \\cos 5x.\n\\]\nCombining like terms, we get:\n\\[\n\\cos 5x = -\\frac{1}{2} \\cos 5x.\n\\]\nAdding \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\cos 5x\\) to both sides, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{2} \\cos 5x = 0.\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n\\cos 5x = 0.\n\\]\nThe smallest positive angle for which \\(\\cos 5x = 0\\) is:\n\\[\n5x = 90^\\circ \\implies x = \\frac{90^\\circ}{5} = 18^\\circ.\n\\]\nTo verify, we check:\n\\[\n\\sin 2 \\cdot 18^\\circ \\sin 3 \\cdot 18^\\circ = \\cos 2 \\cdot 18^\\circ \\cos 3 \\cdot 18^\\circ.\n\\]\nUsing the values of \\(\\sin\\) and \\(\\cos\\) at \\(2 \\cdot 18^\\circ = 36^\\circ\\), \\(3 \\cdot 18^\\circ = 54^\\circ\\), \\(2 \\cdot 18^\\circ = 36^\\circ\\), \\(3 \\cdot 18^\\circ = 54^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\sin 36^\\circ \\sin 54^\\circ = \\cos 36^\\circ \\cos 54^\\circ.\n\\]\nUsing the known trigonometric values, we indeed have:\n\\[\n\\sin 54^\\circ \\sin 36^\\circ = \\cos 54^\\circ \\cos 36^\\circ.\n\\]\nThus, the smallest positive angle \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation is \\(\\boxed{18}\\).",
