    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\\), we can use the trigonometric identity for the cosine of a sum. The identity states that \\(\\cos(A + B) = \\cos A \\cos B - \\sin A \\sin B\\). Rearranging this, we get \\(\\sin A \\sin B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(A-B) - \\cos(A+B)]\\). We can apply this identity to both sides of our equation:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\n\\]\n\nUsing the identity, we can rewrite the left side as:\n\n\\[\n(\\cos(2x-3x) - \\cos(2x+3x)) = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the arguments of the cosine functions, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\cos(-x) - \\cos(5x) = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\cos(-x) = \\cos x\\), the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\cos x - \\cos 5x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\n\\]\n\nWe can use the identity \\(\\cos 5x = \\cos (2x + 3x) = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x - \\sin 2x \\sin 3x\\) to further simplify. However, a more straightforward approach is to notice that if \\(2x = 3x\\), then \\(x = 0\\) is not a solution within the principal range of 0 to 360 degrees. Instead, we can test simple angle values that might satisfy the equation. A good approach is to test \\(x = 5^\\circ\\) and \\(x = 10^\\circ\\) because they are simple multiples of \\(30\\) degrees and 20 degrees respectively.\n\nFirst, let's check \\(x = 5^\\circ\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sin 2(5^\\circ) = \\sin 10^\\circ, \\quad \\cos 2(5^\\circ) = \\cos 10^\\circ, \\quad \\sin 3(5^\\circ) = \\sin 15^\\circ, \\quad \\cos 3(5^\\circ) = \\cos 15^\\circ\n\\]\n\nUsing the values, we see:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 10^\\circ \\sin 15^\\circ = \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 15^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThis holds true. Therefore, the smallest positive angle \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{5}\n\\]",
